@@ -171,8 +171,32 @@ jobs:
       - name: Install the scanners (locked)
         run: uv sync --locked --no-build --only-group scan
       - name: System tools resolve outside the workspace
+        # .venv/bin comes first on PATH (activate-environment), and a locked package can ship a program with a system tool's name.
+        # Pass 1 uses only shell builtins (nothing from PATH runs before it is checked). Pass 2 follows every link with the
+        # now-checked readlink. python3, uv and uvx may sit in .venv/bin by design, but then must be links to a file outside the workspace.
+        # Limit: bash and sh are started by the runner before this step, so the step cannot vouch for them. That is covered by the
+        # locked scan group (uv sync --locked --only-group scan). --no-build only stops a source distribution from being built at install
+        # time; a wheel can still ship a .pth file that runs at Python start, so the lock (exact hashes) is the control, not --no-build.
         run: |
-          for c in sha256sum tar curl git grep date tr cut sort head mktemp rm touch; do p=$(command -v "$c") || exit 1; case "$p" in /*) ;; *) echo "$c is not a file: $p"; exit 1;; esac; case "$p" in "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE"/*) echo "$c resolves inside the workspace: $p"; exit 1;; esac; done
+          sys_tools="readlink sha256sum tar curl git grep date tr cut sort head mktemp rm touch"
+          venv_tools="python3 uv uvx"
+          [ -n "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE" ] || { echo "GITHUB_WORKSPACE is empty"; exit 1; }
+          for c in $sys_tools $venv_tools; do
+            p=$(command -v "$c") || { echo "$c not found"; exit 1; }
+            case "$p" in /*) ;; *) echo "$c is not a file: $p"; exit 1;; esac
+            venv_ok=0
+            case " $venv_tools " in *" $c "*) case "$p" in "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE"/.venv/bin/*) venv_ok=1;; esac;; esac
+            case "$p" in "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE"/*) [ "$venv_ok" = 1 ] || { echo "$c resolves inside the workspace: $p"; exit 1; };; esac
+          done
+          wr=$(readlink -f -- "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE") || exit 1
+          case "$wr" in /*) ;; *) echo "cannot resolve the workspace: $wr"; exit 1;; esac
+          for c in $sys_tools $venv_tools; do
+            p=$(command -v "$c") || exit 1
+            r=$(readlink -f -- "$p") || { echo "cannot resolve $c: $p"; exit 1; }
+            case "$r" in /*) ;; *) echo "cannot resolve $c: $p"; exit 1;; esac
+            case "$r" in "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE"/*|"$wr"/*) echo "$c is a link into the workspace: $p -> $r"; exit 1;; esac
+          done
+          [ -z "$(git ls-files -- .venv)" ] || { echo "files under .venv are tracked"; exit 1; }
       - name: Dependencies with known vulnerabilities (every locked package, hashes required)
         run: |
           uv export --locked --all-extras --all-groups --no-emit-project --format requirements-txt -o "$RUNNER_TEMP/locked.txt"
diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 9107c49..850f022 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,15 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+### CI
+- **Scan-config guard.** `tools/scan_repo.py` (the `check` job) now fails on a comment that switches a scanner or linter off and on any scanner or linter config file, unless it is on the one reviewed list `tools/scan_suppressions.txt`. A review aid until the owner's repository ruleset exists: the list and the code owners file name who must read a change, but GitHub only enforces it once the owner turns on "Require review from Code Owners" (an owner-only setting, not done here).
+  - **What counts as a switch-off comment (matched in any letter case):** the nosec, semgrep ignore, gitleaks allow, trivy ignore, sonar ignore, pmd ignore, checkov skip, tfsec ignore, zizmor ignore, detect-secrets allowlist, lgtm and codeql markers; ruff range switches (`ruff: disable`, `ruff: ignore`, `ruff: enable`); and a `noqa` that is blanket or names a ruff `S` (security) code, a file-level `ruff: noqa` included. Style codes such as `BLE001` are not switch-offs. Prose that only names a tool is not a hit.
+  - **What counts as a config file (by name, in any folder, any letter case):** `.gitleaksignore`, `.gitleaks.toml`, `.semgrepignore`, `.semgrep.yml`, `.trivyignore`, `trivy.yml`, `.bandit`, `.zizmor.yml`, `ruff.toml`, `setup.cfg`, `tox.ini`, `.flake8`, `mypy.ini`, `sonar-project.properties`, `.sonarcloud.properties`, `.coderabbit.yaml`, `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, `.secrets.baseline`, `.snyk`, everything under `.github/codeql/` and `.semgrep/`, every `pyproject.toml` below the repository root, and the two scan list files `tools/scan_allowlist.txt` and `tools/scan_banned_extra.txt`. A below-root `pyproject.toml` that holds a ruff, bandit or semgrep table fails and cannot be listed.
+  - **The list.** One line per file and rule: `path:rule:value:reason`. For a marker rule the value is `count@sha256`: how many markers the file holds and the sha256 of their sorted line texts, so a widened or replaced marker is a change. For a config file it is the sha256 of the file. The list is written with `python tools/scan_repo.py --print-list`, never by hand; a new line carries a placeholder reason that the scan refuses until a reviewer writes a real one (at least 15 characters, not starting with `NEW`). A stale, wrong-count, wrong-hash, reasonless, short-reason, duplicate or unknown-rule line is itself a hit. A glob in the allow list path is refused. The first lines of the list are a baseline taken mechanically from main 2742c1b and were not reviewed one by one.
+  - **Binary files fail closed.** A NUL byte in any file whose extension is not on the short known-binary list (images, archives, fonts, `.whl`, `.parquet`, `.db`, ...) is a hit, and such a file is still scanned as text, so a NUL cannot hide a secret in `.env`, `Dockerfile` or `.html`.
+  - **Also in this change:** the `check` and `tests-py312` jobs are pinned whole in `tests/fixtures/ci_security_golden.json` like the `security` job; the root `pyproject.toml` may not gain a ruff ignore or a bandit section unnoticed; the security job step "System tools resolve outside the workspace" follows links (`readlink -f`), also checks `python3`, `uv` and `uvx` (allowed in `.venv/bin` only as links to a file outside the workspace) and refuses tracked files under `.venv`; `tests/test_scan_repo_mutations.py` plants eight weakenings in a copy of the script and requires the scan tests to fail on each. Three file-level `ruff: noqa` lines were narrowed to per-line ones (`tests/demo_askdata/fuzzgen.py`, `tests/demo_askdata/test_validate_harm.py`); `tests/demo_askdata/corpus.py` keeps its file-level one (S608, ISC004: 188 lines of test SQL text) and is on the list with its reason.
+  - **Upgrade step for a branch:** if your change adds, removes or edits one of these comments or config files, regenerate `tools/scan_suppressions.txt` with `--print-list`, write a reason for every `NEW` line, and commit it in the same PR.
+
 ### Upgrade steps
 - **Breaking: the first stage is now called `demo`; `environment: dev` is refused.** The stages are exactly `demo`, `pilot` and `prod`. A config that says `environment: dev` stops at start with "environment: dev was renamed to demo; write environment: demo (allowed values: demo, pilot, prod)" (shown beside any other problem found in the same file, and also for `Dev`, `DEV` and spellings with spaces, which are refused all the same); any other value (`Demo`, `staging`, `uat`, an empty value) is refused too, and values are never tidied or guessed. To upgrade: (1) change `environment:` in your config to `demo`; (2) if your code imports `backends.table.DEV_ONLY`, import `DEMO_ONLY` instead (same six kind pairs, no old name kept); (3) a project label such as `uat` is not a stage: map it to one stage in your own config. Also: the pilot and prod checks (`deferred.py`, `registry.py`, `app.py`) now ask one function, `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_live(environment)`, which treats anything that is not exactly `demo` as live; the prod-only rules (no local key file, a retention-agreed anchor) ask `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_prod(environment)`, which is true for `prod` and for any value that is not a stage. `STAGES`, `is_live` and `is_prod` live in the new leaf module `hadavin_sdk/stages.py`, not in `config.py`; the list `deferred.LIVE` is removed. The redactor no longer sweeps the words `demo` and `pilot` as a name (it already skipped `prod`), but a secret that reads `demo` is still swept. No stage is stored today; the first change that stores one must map an old `dev` value to `demo` on read, for display only and never for a guard decision, and add a test through that real read site.
 - **Code-quality scanner fixes (SonarCloud, an automated code-quality scanner); one public name removed.** `guard.runmap.PARTIAL_ESCAPE` is gone. If your code imports it, call `guard.runmap.partial_escape_start(text, pos)` instead; it returns the start position as a number (or None), not a match object, so replace a `.search(...)` call and its `.start()` with it. The old pattern looked for a cut-off percent code such as `%C3` at the end of a text and slowed down sharply on a long run of them (about 65 s for 40,000 codes in a row, measured 2026-11-06); the new function takes milliseconds. `guard/http.py` now holds the cloud-metadata addresses that no tool may reach as one documented table `METADATA_ENDPOINTS` (the same six addresses as before; the other refused ranges are unchanged).
